{"id":"Q916","iso2":"AO","iso3":"AGO","name":"Angola","wiki":"Angola","description":"country on the west coast of Southern Africa","flag":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Flag_of_Angola.svg/330px-Flag_of_Angola.svg.png","capital":"Luanda","capitalCoord":[-8.815,13.23],"inception":"1992-08-25","government":["presidential system","republic"],"legislature":["National Assembly of Angola"],"continent":["Africa"],"website":"https://www.governo.gov.ao/","offices":[{"id":"Q846091","role":"hog","title":"President of Angola"},{"id":"Q846091","role":"hos","title":"President of Angola"}],"currentHog":"Q28650390","currentHos":"Q28650390","terms":[{"office":"Q846091","roles":["hog","hos"],"person":"Q181665","start":"1975-11-11","end":"1979-09-10"},{"office":"Q846091","roles":["hog","hos"],"person":"Q1479462","start":"1979-09-11","end":"1979-09-20"},{"office":"Q846091","roles":["hog","hos"],"person":"Q57313","start":"1979-09-21","end":"2017-09-26"},{"office":"Q846091","roles":["hog","hos"],"person":"Q28650390","start":"2017-09-26","end":null}],"people":{"Q181665":{"id":"Q181665","name":"Agostinho Neto","desc":"President of Angola (1975-1979)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/President_MPLA%2C_heer_Neto_door_Den_Uyl_ontvangen_premier_Den_Uyl_en_A_Neto_(r)%2C_Bestanddeelnr_927-8518_(cropped).jpg/330px-President_MPLA%2C_heer_Neto_door_Den_Uyl_ontvangen_premier_Den_Uyl_en_A_Neto_(r)%2C_Bestanddeelnr_927-8518_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1922-09-17","died":"1979-09-10","birthplace":"Catete","parties":["People's Movement for the Liberation of Angola"],"education":["University of Coimbra","University of Lisbon"],"occupations":["politician","poet","physician writer","political prisoner"],"wiki":"Agostinho Neto"},"Q1479462":{"id":"Q1479462","name":"Lúcio Lara","desc":"Angolan politician (1929-2016)","img":null,"gender":"M","born":"1929-04-09","died":"2016-02-27","birthplace":"Caála","parties":["People's Movement for the Liberation of Angola"],"education":["University of Coimbra","University of Lisbon (1911–2013)"],"occupations":["politician","chemist","physicist","mathematician"],"wiki":"Lúcio Lara"},"Q57313":{"id":"Q57313","name":"José Eduardo dos Santos","desc":"President of Angola from 1979 to 2017","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Jos%C3%A9_Eduardo_dos_Santos_2.jpg/330px-Jos%C3%A9_Eduardo_dos_Santos_2.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1942-08-28","died":"2022-07-08","birthplace":"Luanda","parties":["People's Movement for the Liberation of Angola"],"education":["Azerbaijan State Oil and Industrial University"],"occupations":["politician","engineer","minister"],"wiki":"José Eduardo dos Santos"},"Q28650390":{"id":"Q28650390","name":"João Lourenço","desc":"President of Angola since 2017","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/President_of_Angola_Jo%C3%A3o_Louren%C3%A7o_during_a_bilateral_exchange_at_the_Angola_presidential_palace_in_Luanda%2C_Angola%2C_September_27%2C_2023_-_230927-D-PM193-1914_(cropped).jpg/330px-President_of_Angola_Jo%C3%A3o_Louren%C3%A7o_during_a_bilateral_exchange_at_the_Angola_presidential_palace_in_Luanda%2C_Angola%2C_September_27%2C_2023_-_230927-D-PM193-1914_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1954-03-05","died":null,"birthplace":"Lobito","parties":["People's Movement for the Liberation of Angola"],"education":["V. I. Lenin Military-Political Academy"],"occupations":["politician","military personnel","minister","military officer"],"wiki":"João Lourenço"}},"summary":"Angola, officially the Republic of Angola, is a country on the western coast of Southern Africa. It is the second-largest Portuguese-speaking (Lusophone) country after Brazil in both total area and population and is the seventh-largest country in Africa. It is bordered by Namibia to the south, the Democratic Republic of the Congo to the north, Zambia to the east and the Atlantic Ocean to the west. The Angolan province of Cabinda is an exclave on the Atlantic coast, bordering the Republic of the Congo and the Democratic Republic of the Congo. The capital and most populous city is Luanda.\nAngola has been inhabited since the Palaeolithic Age. After the Bantu expansion reached the region, states were formed by the 13th century and organised into confederations. The Kingdom of Kongo ascended to achieve hegemony among the other kingdoms from the 14th century. Portuguese explorers established relations with Kongo in 1483. To the south were the kingdoms of Ndongo and Matamba, with the Ovimbundu kingdoms further south, and the Mbunda Kingdom in the east.\nThe Portuguese began colonising the coast in the 16th century. Kongo fought three wars against the Portuguese, ending in the Portuguese conquest of Ndongo. The banning of the slave trade in the 19th century disrupted Kongo's undiversified economic system and European settlers gradually began to establish their presence in the interior of the region. The Portuguese colony that became Angola did not achieve its present borders until the early 20th century and experienced strong resistance from native groups such as the Cuamato, the Kwanyama, and the Mbunda.\nAfter a protracted anti-colonial struggle (1961–1974), Angola achieved independence in 1975 as a one-party Republic, but the country descended into a devastating civil war the same year, between the ruling People's Movement for the Liberation of Angola (MPLA), backed by the Soviet Union and Cuba; the insurgent National Union for the Total Independence of Angola, an originally Maoist and later anti-communist group supported by the United States and South Africa; the militant organisation National Liberation Front of Angola, backed by Zaire; and the Front for the Liberation of the Enclave of Cabinda seeking the independence of the Cabinda exclave, also backed by Zaire.\nSince the end of the civil war in 2002, Angola has emerged as a relatively stable constitutional republic, and its economy is among the fastest-growing in the world, with China, the European Union, and the United States being the country's largest investment and trade partners. However, the economic growth is highly uneven, with most of the nation's wealth concentrated in a disproportionately small part of the population as most Angolans have a low standard of living; life expectancy is among the lowest in the world, while infant mortality is among the highest.\nAngola is a member of the United Nations, African Union, the Community of Portuguese Language Countries, and the Southern African Development Community. As of 2024, the Angolan population is estimated at 36.6 million. Angolan culture reflects centuries of Portuguese influence, namely the predominance of the Portuguese language and of the Catholic Church, intermingled with a variety of indigenous customs and traditions.","history":"Angola was first settled by San hunter-gatherer societies before the northern domains came under the rule of Bantu states such as Kongo and Ndongo. In the 15th century, Portuguese colonists began trading, and a settlement was established at Luanda during the 16th century. Portugal annexed territories in the region which were ruled as a colony from 1655, and Angola was incorporated as an overseas province of Portugal in 1951. After the Angolan War of Independence, which ended in 1974 with an army mutiny and leftist coup in Lisbon, Angola achieved independence in 1975 through the Alvor Agreement. After independence, Angola entered a long period of civil war that lasted until 2002.","historyTitle":"History of Angola","curated":null,"indicators":{"gdp":[[1980,5930500000],[1981,5550480000],[1982,5550480000],[1983,5784340000],[1984,6131480000],[1985,7554070000],[1986,7072540000],[1987,8084410000],[1988,8769840000],[1989,10201800000],[1990,11229500000],[1991,10603800000],[1992,8307810000],[1993,5768720000],[1994,4438320000],[1995,5538750000],[1996,7526420000],[1997,7648380000],[1998,6506220000],[1999,6152920000],[2000,9129590000],[2001,